  "articleBody": "Upgrading your bathroom with marble, travertine, or precision tile changes the whole feel of your space, but it also comes with tight technical requirements. Natural stone is porous and heavier than ceramic, meaning it demands specific substrate prep, distinct mortar formulations, and routine sealing. Getting a project quote involves more than picking out pretty slabs.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nBefore committing to an estimate, consider how stone behaves in daily moisture. Cement backer board alone is never waterproof; your shower requires a dedicated waterproofing membrane system beneath the stone to protect wall framing. Furthermore, natural stone varies in thickness and absorption, which influences whether thinset or medium-bed mortar is required. If you want large slabs or zero-threshold transitions, your framing must be rigid enough to prevent movement cracks.\n\nQuestions to Ask Before You Decide\nAsk your contractor how they plan to waterproof the wet zones and whether they run a standard 24-hour flood test on the shower pan prior to setting stone. Inquire about their mortar coverage strategy—wet areas require continuous bed coverage without hollow voids behind tiles. \n\nWhen you want clean, durable tile or stone craft, reach out to Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 for a direct consultation across Aurora and Denver.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
